  footer="minimal"
  ogImage="/images/rio-rancho-hero.jpg"
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Rio Rancho"
  nextPage={{ path: '/santa-fe-nm', name: 'Santa Fe' }}
>
  <div class="rr-page">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="hero-photo"></div>
      <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
      <div class="hero-content">
        <span class="hero-city">Rio Rancho, New Mexico</span>
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Foundation Repair</span>
        <h1 class="hero-h1">
          <span class="w1">The</span><span class="w2">City</span>
          <span class="w3">of Vision.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">Foundation repair Rio Rancho, NM — on soil that requires honest attention before anything else.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <div>
            <a href="tel:5059914180" class="hero-phone">(505) 991-4180</a>
            <span class="hero-note">You reach the owner(s)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hero-sep"></div>
          <p class="hero-aside">55 combined years in New Mexico soils.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gold-rule"></div>

    <div class="statement-band">
      <div class="statement-band-inner">
        <span class="statement-band-line1">Foundation Repair</span>
        <span class="statement-band-line2">Rio Rancho.</span>
        <p class="statement-band-secondary">The City of Vision earned its name by looking forward. We look down first.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page-layout">
      <nav class="left-nav">
        <div class="nav-progress"><div class="nav-progress-fill" id="progressFill"></div></div>
        <span class="nav-brand">TLS Foundations · City of Vision</span>
        <ul class="nav-links">
          <li><a href="#what-we-see"><span class="nav-dot"></span>What We See</a></li>
          <li><a href="#history"><span class="nav-dot"></span>History</a></li>
          <li><a href="#geology"><span class="nav-dot"></span>Geology</a></li>
          <li><a href="#construction"><span class="nav-dot"></span>Construction Eras</a></li>
          <li><a href="#approach"><span class="nav-dot"></span>Our Approach</a></li>
          <li><a href="#difference"><span class="nav-dot"></span>The Difference</a></li>
          <li><a href="#questions"><span class="nav-dot"></span>Common Questions</a></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="nav-contact">
          <span class="nav-contact-label">Ready to talk</span>
          <a href="tel:5059914180">(505) 991-4180</a>
          <span class="nav-contact-sub">You reach the owner(s)</span>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <div class="content-area">
        <section class="section" id="what-we-see">
          <span class="section-num">01</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <span class="section-label-text" style="display:block;margin-bottom:8px;">Rio Rancho, New Mexico · What We See</span>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair in Rio Rancho, NM — <em>what's actually happening under your home</em></h2>
            <div class="reveal">
              <p class="lede">Most of what the foundation repair industry knows was built for uniform suburban ground. Rio Rancho sits on one mesa — and that mesa is not uniform.</p>
              <p><mark class="key">Sandy loam in one subdivision, pocket clay in the next, caliche at two feet on one lot and eight feet on the lot beside it.</mark> The variation is real. What it means for a specific home depends entirely on which of those a slab happens to be sitting on.</p>
              <p>In the desert, wet-dry cycles do the work. Decades of monsoon seasons and dry winters move soil, and the practical rule that falls out of that is simple: if it's dry under there, keep it dry. Drainage, grading, and irrigation matter more here than most homeowners are ever told.</p>
              <p>Foundation inspection in Rio Rancho means understanding the ground at a specific address — not running a checklist written for another state.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="history">
          <span class="section-num">02</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">History</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">How a land sale became<br />New Mexico's third largest city</h2>
            <div class="two-col reveal">
              <div>
                <p>Rio Rancho is young. Most of New Mexico measures its history in centuries; this city measures it in decades — and nearly all of it happened within living memory.</p>
                <p>In 1961, AMREP Corporation purchased roughly 55,000 acres of the old Koontz Ranch northwest of Albuquerque and platted it as Rio Rancho Estates. Model homes went up in 1962. The company marketed lots nationally, through sales offices in fourteen states and dinner-party pitches in the north and east, and a great many buyers purchased without ever standing on the land. The city incorporated in 1981 with a population near ten thousand. Intel opened its first plant the same year.</p>
                <p>Sixty-five years on, this is New Mexico's third largest city and one of its fastest growing. It is a good place to live, well run, and still building.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <div class="fact-box">
                  <span class="section-label-text" style="display:block;margin-bottom:14px;color:var(--gold);">City of Vision — By Ordinance</span>
                  <p>"The City of Vision" was adopted in Rio Rancho City Ordinance 87-018. The slogan was submitted by resident Mary Ann Bohl, who won a City Slogan Contest held by the Rio Rancho Chamber of Commerce. It has been in official use since 1987.</p>
                  <p>A city that chose vision as its identity deserves foundation work that actually looks before it acts.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="creed-wrap">
          <div class="creed-card" id="creedCard">
            <a class="creed-title" href="https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0104348/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Glengarry Glen Ross</a>
            <span class="creed-year">1992 · David Mamet</span>
            <p class="creed-note">Four salesmen, one month, a contest where second place gets you fired. <span class="creed-hit">The land they're pushing is called Rio Rancho.</span></p>
            <p class="creed-note">Whether Mamet meant this "City of Vision — Rio Rancho" has never been settled. The real one was sold the same way — by phone, by dinner party, to people who'd never stood on it. That was sixty years ago. The pitch didn't retire.</p>
            <h3 class="creed-line"><span class="creed-word">Always</span><span class="creed-word">Be</span><span class="creed-word">Closing.</span></h3>
          </div>
        </div>

        <section class="section" id="geology">
          <span class="section-num">03</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Geology</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Three soil conditions across <em>one mesa</em></h2>
            <p class="reveal" style="max-width:100%;">Rio Rancho sits on the West Mesa — a broad upland surface west of the Rio Grande, shaped in part by the volcanic activity visible on Albuquerque's western horizon. The surface soil is not a single uniform type. What lies beneath varies by neighborhood, by elevation, by distance from the river corridor, and by the cut-and-fill history of an individual lot.</p>

            <div class="stat-block reveal">
              <div class="stat-item">
                <span class="stat-num" data-target="1961">0</span>
                <span class="stat-label">Land purchased</span>
                <p class="stat-desc">55,000 acres of the Koontz Ranch, platted and sold before the ground was studied lot by lot</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-divider"></div>
              <div class="stat-item">
                <span class="stat-num">3</span>
                <span class="stat-label">Soil conditions<br />on one mesa</span>
                <p class="stat-desc">Sandy loam, caliche, and pocket clay — none of them evenly distributed</p>
              </div>
              <div class="stat-divider"></div>
              <div class="stat-item">
                <span class="stat-num" data-target="55">0<span>+</span></span>
                <span class="stat-label">Combined years<br />in NM soils</span>
                <p class="stat-desc">TLS Foundations and Sandia GEO combined</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flip-cards reveal">
              <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="West Mesa sandy loam — tap to learn more">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">1</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">West Mesa Sandy Loam</div>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">Soil Condition 01</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">Most of Rio Rancho's residential land sits on sandy loam. It drains relatively well and behaves predictably when moisture conditions stay stable. The risk is differential settlement when they don't — irrigation added, drainage changed, a wet decade following a dry one. Sandy loam is the baseline, not the problem.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">Foundation settlement · Rio Rancho NM</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Caliche subsoil — tap to learn more">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">2</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">Caliche Subsoil</div>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">Soil Condition 02</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">Caliche — hardened calcium carbonate — runs through the mesa at varying depths. Shallow, consistent caliche can be a perfectly good bearing layer. The problem is inconsistency: two feet under one part of a slab, eight feet under another. Two materials, one foundation, uneven movement.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">Caliche · differential settlement Rio Rancho</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Pocket clays — tap to learn more">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">3</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">Pocket Clays</div>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">Soil Condition 03</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">Expansive clay occurs in pockets across the mesa — not uniformly, and not visible at the surface. It absorbs moisture and expands, dries and contracts, through every monsoon and every dry winter. Toward the Rio Grande corridor, finer alluvial deposits make clay-bearing soil more likely and its response to moisture more pronounced.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">Expansive clay · foundation heave Rio Rancho NM</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="pull reveal">The West Mesa doesn't have one soil. It has several. The only way to know which one is under a specific home is to look.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="construction">
          <span class="section-num">04</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Construction History</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Sixty years of building, <em>and still growing into it</em></h2>
            <p class="reveal" style="max-width:680px;">Rio Rancho went from empty mesa to New Mexico's third largest city in about the span of one working career. That pace is the single most useful thing to understand about its housing stock — because the construction era of a home tells you what practices were standard when the ground under it was prepared.</p>

            <div class="growth-chart reveal">
              <span class="growth-chart-label">Rio Rancho population · U.S. Census</span>
              <div class="chart-bars" id="chartBars">
                <div class="chart-col" data-pct="9"><span class="chart-figure">9,985</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
                <div class="chart-col" data-pct="28"><span class="chart-figure">32,551</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
                <div class="chart-col" data-pct="45"><span class="chart-figure">51,765</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
                <div class="chart-col" data-pct="76"><span class="chart-figure">87,521</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
                <div class="chart-col" data-pct="91"><span class="chart-figure">104,046</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
                <div class="chart-col est" data-pct="100"><span class="chart-figure">114,419</span><div class="chart-bar"></div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="chart-axis">
                <span class="chart-year">1980</span>
                <span class="chart-year">1990</span>
                <span class="chart-year">2000</span>
                <span class="chart-year">2010</span>
                <span class="chart-year">2020</span>
                <span class="chart-year">2025</span>
              </div>
              <div class="chart-eras">
                <div class="chart-era"><span class="chart-era-name">AMREP tract era</span><span class="chart-era-note">1963 – 1980s</span></div>
                <div class="chart-era"><span class="chart-era-name">Intel era</span><span class="chart-era-note">1980s – 2000s</span></div>
                <div class="chart-era"><span class="chart-era-name">Master-planned communities</span><span class="chart-era-note">2000s – present</span></div>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-source">Decennial census counts 1980–2020. 2025 figure is the U.S. Census Bureau population estimate for July 1, 2025. The 1980 count predates incorporation, which occurred in 1981.</span>
            </div>

            <h3>The AMREP tract era</h3>
            <p>The original tract homes were production-built at scale across the numbered units of the AMREP plat. Conventional slab-on-grade construction on ground that had not been characterized parcel by parcel. These are ordinary homes, most of them performing normally after four to six decades — but they are also the housing stock with the longest exposure to whatever the soil beneath them happens to be, and the longest history of drainage and irrigation changes made by successive owners.</p>

            <h3>The Intel era</h3>
            <p>Intel's arrival brought the steepest population climb on the chart above. That wave produced more conventional suburban construction — larger footprints, more consistent building practice, better documented. Foundation questions in this stock tend to be site-specific rather than era-specific, and drainage is the recurring theme. Flat mesa terrain means water does not always leave a lot on its own.</p>

            <h3>Master-planned communities</h3>
            <p>Cabezon, Mariposa, Loma Colorado, Enchanted Hills and the newer developments on the west and north sides were built with engineered drainage and modern grading requirements. That is a genuine improvement. It does not change what is underneath — pocket clay and variable caliche exist on the mesa regardless of when a home was built on top of them.</p>

            <div class="pull reveal">The era tells you what was standard practice. The soil tells you what it was built on. A repair recommendation needs both.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="approach">
          <span class="section-num">05</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Our Approach</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation investigation in Rio Rancho, NM — <em>before everything else</em></h2>
            <div class="two-col reveal">
              <div>
                <p>That instruction never left the business. It just changed products. A rep at the door, a number before the tape measure is back in the truck, a signature requested the same afternoon. We have seen estimates arrive with a discount attached that expires if you don't sign that day. ABC.</p>
                <p><mark class="key">Here is what a same-day quote cannot account for: which of three soil conditions is under that specific slab, at what depth, and what the drainage has been doing to it for the last thirty years.</mark> A sandy loam lot and a pocket clay lot two streets apart are different problems with different answers. Nobody can tell them apart from a driveway.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <div class="pull">We would rather tell you nothing is wrong than sell you something that is.</div>
                <p>TLS Foundations works with Sandia GEO, a New Mexico geotechnical investigation firm, when site conditions warrant a formal soil investigation before repair is recommended. Sometimes the honest answer is drainage correction. Sometimes it is monitoring and nothing else. The investigation is what tells you which.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="difference">
          <span class="section-num">06</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Why Rio Rancho Is Different</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Three letters, <em>used differently</em></h2>
            <p class="lede reveal">This city was built by people who were very good at closing. We took the same three letters and pointed them the other direction.</p>

            <div class="flip-cards reveal">
              <div class="flip-card abc" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="A — Always assess first. Tap to learn more.">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">A</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">Always assess first.</div>
                      <p class="flip-front-sub">Before a recommendation. Before a number. Before anything.</p>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">A · Assessment</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">The soil under a home is the only thing that makes an estimate mean anything. Sandy loam here, pocket clay there, caliche at two feet or eight depending on where you're standing. TLS works with Sandia GEO when a formal soil investigation is warranted — before any repair method is recommended, not after.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">Investigation first</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flip-card abc" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="B — Before you sign, know your soil. Tap to learn more.">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">B</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">Before you sign, know your soil.</div>
                      <p class="flip-front-sub">Anyone can quote a slab. Almost nobody quotes what's under it.</p>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">B · Before You Sign</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">Ask any contractor what the soil is doing at your address and how they know. If the answer is a discount that expires tonight, that is a sales tactic, not a diagnosis. Get the soil answer first. The estimate means something once you have it — and not before.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">Diagnosis before discount</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flip-card abc" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="C — Close the door on the one-visit close. Tap to learn more.">
                <div class="flip-card-inner">
                  <div class="flip-front">
                    <span class="flip-num">C</span>
                    <div>
                      <div class="flip-title">Close the door on the one-visit close.</div>
                      <p class="flip-front-sub">The only thing that should close on a first visit is the list of what we still need to find out.</p>
                      <span class="flip-hint">Tap to learn more</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flip-back">
                    <span class="flip-tag">C · Close the Right Way</span>
                    <p class="flip-body">55 combined years working New Mexico soils. Owner-operated, which means the person who looks at your home is the person who answers the phone. No scheduler, no commissioned rep, no expiring offer. If your foundation is fine, we will tell you that and leave.</p>
                    <span class="flip-tag">TLS Foundations · Rio Rancho, NM</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="questions">
          <span class="section-num">07</span>
          <div class="section-inner">
            <div class="section-label"><span class="section-label-bar"></span><span class="section-label-text">Common Questions · Rio Rancho, NM</span></div>
            <h2 class="section-heading reveal">Foundation repair questions <em>Rio Rancho homeowners ask</em></h2>
            <p class="reveal" style="max-width:680px;">Methods, costs, and what to expect — answered for this market specifically, not in general.</p>

            <div class="seo-accordion reveal">
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation repair Rio Rancho NM — where do you start?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">With the ground, not the crack. Rio Rancho sits on West Mesa soils that change across short distances — sandy loam, caliche at variable depth, and pocket clays that are not visible at the surface. Until we know which of those is under a specific slab and at what depth, any repair recommendation is a guess. TLS Foundations investigates first. Call (505) 991-4180 — you reach the owner(s).</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Slab repair Rio Rancho NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Nearly all Rio Rancho residential construction is slab-on-grade, so slab repair is the most common question in this market. The right approach depends on the mechanism: a slab settling into a soft zone needs a different answer than a slab being lifted by clay expansion. Those two look similar from inside the house and require opposite responses. Identifying which one is happening is the first job.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation repair cost Rio Rancho NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Cost follows cause. A drainage correction and a pier system are separated by an order of magnitude, and both get recommended for the same set of symptoms by different companies. If you are collecting estimates in Rio Rancho and they vary wildly with no soil evaluation behind any of them, that spread is not telling you about your house — it is telling you about the companies.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Helical piers Rio Rancho NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Helical piers transfer structural load to a deeper, competent bearing layer. On the West Mesa, the depth and consistency of caliche is the governing question — shallow, continuous caliche changes the design significantly compared to caliche that appears at eight feet on one side of a home and two on the other. Pier selection without knowing that profile is a specification written on assumption.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Push piers Rio Rancho NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Push piers rely on the weight of the structure to drive to resistance. That works when there is reliable bearing at a reachable depth and the structure is heavy enough to develop it. In variable West Mesa profiles, whether that condition exists is a site question, not a product question. It is one of the reasons we characterize soil before selecting between pier types rather than after.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Concrete lifting and foam injection Rio Rancho</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Lifting a settled driveway, patio, or garage slab is often the right call in Rio Rancho, and it is far less invasive than structural repair. The caution is straightforward: if the soil that caused the settlement is still active — an unaddressed drainage problem, an ongoing moisture source — lifting the concrete without correcting the cause buys time rather than a result.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Drainage correction and grading Rio Rancho</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">On flat mesa terrain, water does not leave a lot on its own. Rio Rancho's older subdivisions frequently have minimal drainage design, and decades of irrigation, added landscaping, and re-grading by successive owners change the moisture picture around a foundation. In a meaningful share of the homes we look at here, drainage correction is the actual recommendation — not piers.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation settlement Rio Rancho NM</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Settlement in Rio Rancho is usually differential rather than uniform — one part of a structure moving relative to another, driven by a change in what the soil is doing under one portion of the slab. That is why the pattern of symptoms across a house matters more than any single crack. Where the movement is concentrated tells you a great deal about what is happening below.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Are stucco cracks a foundation problem?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Not necessarily. Stucco cracks for several reasons, including normal curing and thermal movement, and most hairline cracking in Rio Rancho is cosmetic. What raises the question is pattern and behavior: cracks that widen over time, reopen after being patched, run diagonally from the corners of doors and windows, or appear alongside sticking doors and uneven floors. One symptom is noise. Several together is a signal.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Is my newer Rio Rancho home safe from foundation problems?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Newer construction in the master-planned communities was built with engineered drainage and modern grading standards, which genuinely reduces risk. But the soil beneath is the same mesa. Pocket clay does not check the build date. Age of home is useful context for what practices were standard — it is not a verdict either direction.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Do I need a soil investigation, or just a contractor?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">Not every home needs one. Many Rio Rancho questions resolve with a careful look at drainage, grading, and the pattern of movement inside the house. A formal geotechnical investigation is warranted when the answer genuinely turns on what the soil profile is doing at depth — and in those cases the cost of the investigation is small against the cost of installing the wrong system. TLS works with Sandia GEO when a site reaches that threshold.</div></div>
              </div>
              <div class="seo-item">
                <button class="seo-trigger"><span class="seo-trigger-text">Foundation inspection Rio Rancho NM — what happens?</span><span class="seo-trigger-icon">+</span></button>
                <div class="seo-drawer"><div class="seo-drawer-inner">We look at the exterior grading and drainage, the pattern and location of cracking inside and out, door and window operation throughout the house, floor elevations where warranted, and the history of the site — irrigation, plumbing leaks, landscaping changes, and what the neighbors have experienced. Then we tell you what we think is happening and what, if anything, is worth doing about it. That last part sometimes ends with "nothing yet."</div></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="contact-closer reveal">
          <div class="contact-closer-inner">
